  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThis one of those strings of shifts when I feel like I have Deja Vu \nevery night (the feeling, not the 1980 hit by Dionne Warwick). \nBasically, the weather is the same every night and the possibly \nhazards are the same every night. And this is continues tonight, \nwith the heat and fire the concerns.\n\nAs for today, we will have very warm temperatures. However, there is \none other thing that may have an impact, the smoke. It could help \nkeep temperatures a few degrees warmer than what would be expected. \nHowever, it still looks like a hot day with most locations below \n6300 feet above 90 degrees for a high temperature. As for the \ncentury mark (100 degrees), the highest chance is across central \nWyoming below 5000 feet, in places like Riverton, Shoshoni and even \nthe lower elevations of Casper. We will likely have some record \nhighs fall today. The other concern is for fire weather. Most \nguidance is showing somewhat lighter wind than today, especially \nEast of the Divide. It is still rather gusty across southern Wyoming \nthough, with a greater than 1 in 2 chance of gusts past 25 mph. With \nthat, we will issue a Red Flag Warning for Sweetwater, Sublette, \nsouthern Lincoln and southeastern Fremont County. We also included \nthe eastern Wind River Range. I did give thought to the Wind River \nBasin. However, wind looks too borderline at this time. Also, the \nweekend looks more concerning so we lift it out for now.\n\nIt is more of the same for the weekend, with strong ridging \nremaining over the Cowboy State. The very hot temperatures will \ncontinue, with 700 millibar temperatures climbing over 20 celsius \nfor some locations. Saturday looks like the warmest day of the two, \nwith most locations below 5500 feet having at least a 1 in 2 chance \nof highs of 100 or higher. Sunday still looks hot, but maybe a \ndegree or two cooler. However, there does look to be more of a \nbreeze this days, so the chance of critical fire weather also looks \nto increase. We may need more fire weather highlights this weekend, \nbut will will punt the decision to the day shift for now. As for \nconvection, the chance remains very low. A bit of mid level moisture \nmay try to creep into far southwestern Wyoming on Saturday, but it \nis the mid level variety so the only activity may be a few virga \nshowers given the very dry low levels. The chance may be slightly \nhigher on Sunday afternoon but probably no more than 1 out of 6 and \nagain, largely confined to southwestern Wyoming. And, we will likely \ncontinue to deal with areas of smoke that will reduce visibility at \ntimes.\n\nAs we head into next week, the ridge will begin to retreat to the \nsouth, and this should allow for somewhat cooler temperatures. \nHowever, temperatures still look to be above normal. It could allow \nfor some more monsoonal moisture to spread into the area though. \nThis morning runs did come in somewhat drier though, keeping the \nbulk of the moisture across Utah and Colorado with Wyoming only on \nthe edge of the best moisture. As a result, coverage looks rather \nsparse next week with at most a 1 out of 3 chance for any given \narea. There has also been a lack of model consistency, so trying to \npick out a more active day or placement of any convection is nearly \nimpossible this far out. There some signals it could be more active \nnext weekend, but a lot can change this far out. So, as for the next \nthree days, it looks like more of the same. Near record to record \nhigh temperatures, little to no chance of rain and elevated to \ncritical fire weather.",
